{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST be the home of deriving an initial requirement set from existing code.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject (`requirements.cli.skill_structure.bootstrap`) has one obligation. Under `requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill must be the home of deriving an initial requirement set from existing code. That means the topic is stated in full there, and, per the parent's \"exactly one skill\" rule, not stated in another skill.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_bootstrap_skill_is_home_of_deriving_an_initial_requirement_set]` is marked {test} for the subject. It covers the obligation as follows:\n\n- **Stated in full in the home skill.**\n  - The test requires the `## Inventories`, `## Proposing requirements`, `## Tagging` and `## Report` sections to exist in `SKILL.md`. The `section` helper asserts that each heading is present.\n  - It asserts the content of three of them. `## Proposing requirements` must contain \"Write one requirement per obligation a user could rely on\" and \"Label each proposal and act on the label\". `## Tagging` must contain the `{test}`-on-tests and `{impl}`-on-roots rule and the rule that maps each other inventoried item to the requirement whose root reaches it. `## Report` must contain the four fields `Tests mapped:`, `Items mapped:`, `Independent recount:` and `Gaps:`.\n  - `assert_home` calls `assert_phrases` on the whole bundled skill with the `home_markers` for this skill. Those markers are \"every first-party runnable test or test case\" (inventories), \"## Proposing requirements\", and \"Independent recount:\". That covers the inventory content as well.\n- **Exactly one home.** `assert_home` goes through every other skill in `SKILLS`. It builds each one with `bundled_skill`, which joins `SKILL.md` and its `references/` files, and asserts that none contains any of this skill's markers. A restatement of the topic in another skill or its references would therefore fail the test.\n\nThe `SKILLS` list covers all five bundled skills. `skill_files` includes each skill's `references/` files, which matches the parent's definition of a skill.\n\nI could not read the body text of `SKILL.md`, because the node reads returned only elided text. The assessment therefore relies on the test's assertions and helpers. It judges whether those assertions enforce the requirement, not whether the current skill text passes them.\n\nThe assertions are specific enough that a skill missing a section, a core instruction or a report field would fail. They also fail if the topic's key phrases appear in another skill. I found no obligation or case in the text that is left unchecked, and no marking gap.",
  "references": "b02e72a8f64a2f9a1f68893267db6677c2ecf07ab8202182d024a68119169ae3",
